ourself  honestly i have trouble thinking more  than a couple years out  um because i i realized a while ago  that it's nice to have good ambitious  goals whatever that might be most of  those for me  are not necessarily career related yeah  and  i really enjoy what i do i love the  process of learning i love the process  of  figuring out what's next or and then  foreseeable six months two years out  and um you know i i've  become a lot more interested in  evaluating  opportunities as they come rather than  trying to  kind of strong-arm opportunities so i  could be in any of those roles  realistically if i felt like it was a  great fit  or it was a a good match for me  at the current time so i know that's not  exactly a great answer  um in terms of where i see the data  science field going in general  i think that it's probably going to  diverge a little bit so we'll see  more data scientists that are focused on  business understanding and  implementation  more to consult consulting capacity  helping to under the business side  understand  what's going on in the technical side  and there'll be another  kind of branch that is more focused on  the  machine learning engineering and the  heavily technical implementation side  um i don't think that the algorithm side  is going to go  is going to ever not be important but we  have really good packages there's  advances in auto ml there's a lot of  advancements  in tools that help you train and create  models  and so that element of data science i  don't think is going to be as prevalent  you know  it's going to be a lot more about  applying the models that we have  then building new models or anything  along those lines so  it'll i think we'll have kind of a  software engineering  data scientist and a consulting style  data scientist that's kind of my  how i see the future and who knows where  i'll be  interesting yeah i couldn't agree more  with that  perspective that eventually we're going  to have divergence because right now the  field  there's too much going on in there i  mean to our point earlier about how  there's different  breeds of data scientists yeah there's  so much going on with needing to  to know all the technical sides of  things  um how to tell a compelling story the  business side of things  stats like i get it there's a lot there  so  to that point would you say let's say  somebody's in school right now or  they're very early on in their career  and they're a very  very technical person do you think that  person has anything to be afraid of  about their job being automated  or their job being like something like  auto ml  just reaching a much higher level of  maturity and kind of  kind of getting to where they don't have  a job anymore does that make sense  do you think that that's a legitimate  concern or do you think that's one of  those overblown  things that's just put up there for uh  for people to make click made articles  based off of  i think it's way overblown i mean we  still have a ton of software engineers  we look at something let's take a  completely different field like  accounting  right we have software that should be  able to automate  all accounting right now right in theory  we should be able to completely  remove that at a corporate level at any  level  there's still plenty of accountants i  don't think the field is is losing  uh credibility or there's any less  accountants than there used to be  right i mean we've had technology to  i mean turbo tax does it a little bit  but we've had technology that should  be able to automate a lot of this for  the last 10 years easily and  i don't think that there's any real  change in the number of accountants or  anything like that there's still roles  they're still hiring new people  because there's nuance within the types  of accounting there's  there's things that are edge cases  there's  uh i would assume as  company or corporate infrastructure  evolves there's new types  of things that have to change within  accounting as well and data science is  no different you know there's different  tools that come up  someone's going to have to pull the  lever at the very least to get these  algorithms to run and they're going to  want people that have an understanding  of how  how these things work so if something  goes wrong you can diagnose it you can  fix it  so maybe it's going to be less hands-on  but it's the very least going to be  oversight  yeah no i i basically 100 agree with  that i hadn't thought about the  the whole accounting and taxes thing and  how that's not been automated  i mean to a to a great extent i really  haven't thought about it before in those  terms so that's a really  that's a really interesting analogy  there  all right so we're hitting uh the top of  the hour here so  i actually have one final question for  you and this is a big one you ready  hit me all right and that is what is  your  single biggest tip for somebody just  getting into this field for the first  time  so somebody very early in their career  or somebody who's just coming out of  school  they want to get into data science and  make a bit a  big fulfilling career out of it what's  your one tip for that person  so i think my biggest tip is to  realize that you're capable of growth  i've talked we talked about this before  i've talked about before with a lot of  people it's the idea of a growth mindset  and to realize just because you don't  know something now doesn't mean that  tomorrow or in a week or in a month  you'll be able to pick it up and  completely understand it and look back  and say oh that was pretty easy now i  get it  right there are plenty of concepts that  i learned and when i was starting out  i was completely overwhelmed i thought  that this would be impossible for me to  pick up  and i look back now and they're it's so  simple to me  and that's something that a lot of  people  lose faith in is that they really they  think that they're not good enough or  